WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.675
Relational databases are best known for structured
rows and columns but PostgreSQL

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:10.012
can also work with semi-structured JSON data One
useful PostgreSQL type is

00:00:10.025 --> 00:00:15.162
JSONB In our clinical_notes table the note column
stores values such as

00:00:15.188 --> 00:00:21.212
symptom severity and follow_up inside a JSON object
The arrow-arrow operator

00:00:21.438 --> 00:00:25.450
written as two greater-than symbols after the hyphen
extracts a JSON value

00:00:25.475 --> 00:00:30.638
as text For example note arrow-arrow symptom returns
the symptom value as

00:00:30.662 --> 00:00:34.950
text We give the extracted value an alias so the
result has

00:00:34.975 --> 00:00:39.900
a clean column name For follow_up we want a Boolean
value rather

00:00:39.912 --> 00:00:45.300
than text The expression ends with double colon
boolean That is a

00:00:45.312 --> 00:00:50.488
PostgreSQL cast It converts the extracted text into
a Boolean that can

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.500
be treated as true or false The WHERE clause filters
notes whose

00:00:54.525 --> 00:00:58.988
severity is moderate or severe by using IN The
result is then

00:00:59.012 --> 00:01:04.000
ordered by patient ID Run the query and inspect the
extracted fields

00:01:04.862 --> 00:01:08.338
Notice that the JSON values behave like columns in
the result even

00:01:08.362 --> 00:01:12.400
though they are stored inside one JSONB column JSONB
is useful when

00:01:12.425 --> 00:01:16.388
some data has a flexible structure but it should not
automatically replace

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.925
normal relational columns The design choice depends
on the data and how

00:01:20.938 --> 00:01:21.888
you need to query it
